<html>
<title>Pseudo Debugger</title>
<body>
<h1>Pseudo Debugger</h1>

<p>
The Pseudo Debugger allows tracing and control of pseudo-code.
</p>

<p>
The main elements of the Pseudo Debugger are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Debugger</strong> tab</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Pseudo</strong> sub-tab</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Pseudo Type</strong> field</li>
</ul>

<p>
To open this tab, select the <strong>Debugger</strong> tab (1) and then the <strong>Pseudo</strong> sub-tab (2).
</p>

<p>
The type of pseudo-code is shown in the <strong>Pseudo Type</strong> field (3).
</p>


<h2>Debug Mode</h2>

<p>
Debug mode enables instruction tracing and debugging controls.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_debugmode.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Debug Mode</strong> checkbox</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Trace</strong> and <strong>INT</strong> buttons</li>
</ul>

<p>
Enable debug mode using the <strong>Debug Mode</strong> checkbox (1). Using the <strong>Trace</strong> or <strong>INT</strong> buttons (2) also activates debug mode.
</p>

<p>
When debug mode is active, this is indicated in the title of the emulated computer window.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Debug mode slows the emulator and increases host CPU usage.
</p>


<h2>Tracing</h2>

<p>
The Trace section controls instruction tracing and stepping through program execution.
</p>

<p>
The tracing controls are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_trace.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Trace</strong> button - when enabled all pseudo instructions are shown.</li>
<li>(2) <strong>INT</strong> button - when enabled CDP1802 interrupt instructions are shown.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Trace Window</h2>

<p>
Trace output appears in the trace window (1) shown below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_trace_window.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) Trace window</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Log</strong> button - saves the trace output to a file.</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Clear</strong> button - clears the trace window.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Execution Control</h2>

<p>
Execution control buttons are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_PSS.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Pause</strong> button - pauses program execution, second press resumes.</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Step</strong> button - executes the specified number of pseudo instructions.</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Speed</strong> slider - controls execution speed while debugging.</li>
</ul>

<p>
If the <strong>Pause</strong> button is pressed during execution of a pseudo-code instruction, pseudo-code pauses only when the current instruction finishes. During execution of the current instruction, the <strong>Pause</strong> button is disabled. This is especially visible when pausing during a command such as <tt>LD Vx, K</tt> in Chip-8 - the pause action takes effect only after a key press.
</p>


<h2>Pseudo Registers</h2>

<p>
Pseudo register control buttons are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_registers.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) Variables display</li>
<li>(2) <strong>I</strong> text field</li>
<li>(3) <strong>PC</strong> text field</li>
<li>(4) <strong>Protected</strong> checkbox</li>
</ul>

<p>
The current values of all variables (1), the I register (2) and the PC register (3) are displayed.
</p>

<p>
The <strong>Protected</strong> checkbox (4) is enabled by default, preventing value edits that could crash the emulator. To edit values (except the PC register), uncheck <strong>Protected</strong> (4).
</p>

<p>
To change a value, type the new value and press <tt>ENTER</tt>. Changes apply only after pressing <tt>ENTER</tt>. Pause the emulator before editing values to avoid unexpected behaviour.
</p>


<h2>Breakpoints</h2>

<p>
Breakpoints allow execution to pause automatically when a specified address is reached.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debug_pseudo_break.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Breakpoint</strong> text field and <strong>Set</strong> button</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Breakpoint</strong> field</li>
</ul>

<p>
Enter an execution address in the <strong>Breakpoint</strong> text field (1) and press <tt>ENTER</tt> or the <strong>Set</strong> button; execution pauses when it reaches this address. All set breakpoints appear in the list (2) and can be deleted, deactivated, or edited.
</p>


<h3>Delete</h3>

<p>
Select a breakpoint in the list and press the <tt>DELETE</tt> key to remove it.
</p>


<h3>Deactivate</h3>

<p>
(Windows only) Uncheck a breakpoint checkbox to deactivate it. Check it again to reactivate.
</p>


<h3>Edit</h3>

<p>
Select a breakpoint in the list and edit the text directly to modify it.
</p>

</body>
</html>
